\title{Constructible regular polygons}
\taxon{Corollary}
\date{2023-10-01}
\author{JB00}
\import{macros}

\p{A regular #{n}-gon is constructible by ruler and compass iff #{n} is a power of #{2} times distinct primes of the form #{F_k = 2^{2^k} + 1}.}

\proof{
\p{If #{n = \prod p_i^{\alpha_i}} then #{\phi(n) = \prod p_i^{\alpha_i - 1}(p_i - 1)}. So #{\phi(n)} is a power of #{2} #{\iff} #{n} is a product of a power of #{2} and distinct odd primes of the form #{2^m + 1}.}

\p{If #{2^m + 1} is prime then #{m} must be a power of #{2}. Indeed, if #{m = ab} with #{b > 1} odd, then putting #{x = 2^a} in}

##{x^b + 1 = (x + 1)(x^{b-1} - x^{b-2} + \cdots - x + 1)}

\p{gives a nontrivial factorisation.}
}
